<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingayen Gulf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lingayen Gulf is a large inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, bordered by Pangasinan and La Union. Its coast was home to the trading polity of Caboloan and was visited by Japanese and Chinese merchants. Japanese forces landed there in December 1941, and American forces landed there in January 1945 to retake Luzon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lingayen-gulf.jpg" alt="Lingayen Gulf" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P199 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Fabian_Pangasinan_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lingayen Gulf stretches 56 km along north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> and is framed by the provinces of Pangasinan and La Union. It lies between the Zambales Mountains and the Cordillera Central. Five major rivers flow into it: the Agno, Dagupan and Angalacan-Bued rivers from Pangasinan in the south, and the Aringay and Bauang rivers from La Union in the east. The gulf holds numerous islands, the best known of which are in the Hundred Islands National Park, with 123 islands, most of them small. The largest island is Cabarruyan Island, which forms the municipality of Anda, Pangasinan. The shore from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a> to San Fabian is a long grey-sand beach, and the waters are murky because of the sandy bottom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the gulf was a strategically important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> between American and Japanese forces. On 22 December 1941 the Japanese 14th Army under Lieutenant General Masaharu Homma landed on the eastern part of the gulf at San Fernando City, Bauang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, Caba, Aringay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoo">Agoo</a> and Santo Tomas. The defenders, a poorly equipped force of mostly Filipino and American troops, fought minor skirmishes and the Japanese occupied the gulf. The next day General MacArthur ordered a retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> to Bataan, and the gulf stayed under Japanese occupation for three years.</p><p>At 09:30 on 9 January 1945 the U.S. 6th Army made an amphibious landing after a heavy naval bombardment, with 68,000 troops ashore on the first day and 203,608 in all along a beachhead of 32 km from Sual, Lingayen and Dagupan in the west to San Fabian in the east. Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a> attacks caused heavy losses: from 13 December 1944 to 13 January 1945, 24 ships were sunk and 67 damaged. Afterwards the gulf became a vast supply depot for the assaults on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, and for Okinawa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The cities along the coast include Dagupan and Alaminos in Pangasinan, Lingayen, the capital of Pangasinan, and San Fernando City, the capital of La Union. Fishing and salt-making are the main industries; the name Pangasinan means place where salt is made, and salt is collected from seawater by evaporation. Pangasinan observes 9 January as Veterans' Day, established in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lingayen Gulf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The people lived around the <b>Lingayen Gulf</b> and along the Agno River, with their centre at Binalatongan, later San Carlos.</blockquote><a href="/state/caboloan">Caboloan</a></li></ul></section>
